<p style="margin: 0 0 20px 0; color: #1f2d3d; font-size: 22px; font-weight: bold; text-align: left; line-height: 1.35;">Email multisig is now required for all blockchain users</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0; color: #3a4a5c; font-size: 16px; text-align: left; line-height: 1.55;">Hello,</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0; color: #3a4a5c; font-size: 16px; text-align: left; line-height: 1.55;">We are writing to let you know about a change that now applies across the network. From this point, an email address must be signed on the smart contract of your wallet and a second sign method must be set up beside it. Together, these two items form what we call Email Multisig.</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0; color: #3a4a5c; font-size: 16px; text-align: left; line-height: 1.55;">Email Multisig links your wallet to a mailbox you control. Once enabled, the smart contract recognizes both your wallet and your email as proof of ownership. Every action that changes the state of your smart contract is then signed by the two together, so a single key is no longer enough on its own.</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0; color: #3a4a5c; font-size: 16px; text-align: left; line-height: 1.55;">What this means in practice: any blockchain user, and any dApp wallet holder, can enable Email Multisig from the page linked below. MetaMask is one supported wallet, but the same flow works for other wallets that support smart contract calls. After Email Multisig is enabled, there is no need to open MetaMask again to sign future transactions, and no need to open any other dApp either. Your email signature carries the verification on the smart contract from that point on.</p>

<div style="text-align: center; margin: 28px 0 28px 0;"><a style="display: inline-block; padding: 12px 36px; background-color: #0d3b3a; color: #ffffff; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 4px; font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;" href="https://track.pstmrk.it/3s/u2330170.ct.sendgrid.net%2Fls%2Fclick%3Fupn%3Du001.cnuNyO8lRY3x2ZSFqOUMQaLxSylIuEDULL7r-2FY1CCE8Zqp3-2BTzgFgK9MsQjxffoTw2Fc_6-2BTvvr0eQsYTzD4i-2BkUEXHV0c4Kt36vBHG1FDyaqpMSw8-2Ba4ZfLJznTbrjn-2FjZ4CdWYj0B3fohrmnG46yUbLPkLZWBTDaZpq7rV4wudeUnJ-2FvpOTXqv8wqUkh54b-2BM08iGQ9sfiBx9OaVuHj9pt6W2T4YJtL-2F3PjqT8NKsJzB2MdLLtGNJBd-2BScddvbFotQAkGF6cfVVnMZKEw959X8JiD5DakckN-2BB7Y-2BynVNe74yFcNy9HHsRDdzfTG8gN-2Bhnd/B3L0/cEnIAQ/AQ/49957a17-c24f-4238-b512-329b164caab2/1/G35xd2JgLW?r981d2fb8=CHxqt-cwYVpez_R3XsFTHzQTH6yk2miKVG0m8BbdVT00zGguef1l4sEu&amp;kff6fc3de=-oxjCb37zJWKv9dF&amp;x15f837a8=7262613">Enable Email Multisig</a></div>

<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0; color: #3a4a5c; font-size: 16px; text-align: left; line-height: 1.55;">Alongside this change, all supported networks are opening their support doors to every blockchain user. Whether you hold a wallet that has never been linked to a mailbox, or you use a dApp wallet that has not yet been updated, the flow is the same. You confirm the email address on record, add a second sign method, and enable Email Multisig on your contract in a single visit.</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 24px 0; color: #3a4a5c; font-size: 16px; text-align: left; line-height: 1.55;">The change takes effect for all blockchain users by <strong>28 September</strong>. The page linked above remains open for the next 24 hours so you can complete both steps in one session.</p>

<p style="margin: 0; color: #3a4a5c; font-size: 15px; text-align: left; line-height: 1.55;">If anything needs a hand, our support team is one message away. Thank you for reading.</p>

